<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zaydism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zaydi Shia Islam, Zaydi Shi'ism, Zaydiyya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zaydism is a branch of Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> named after Zayd ibn Ali, a great-grandson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Ali, who led a revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> in 740. Zaydis accept as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> any qualified descendant of Hasan or Husayn who claims the office and takes up arms, and they stand close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> in law. Zaydi states arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> in the 9th century and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, where Zaydi imams ruled until 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zaydism.jpg" alt="Zaydism" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.Schoettler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yemen-2005-IMG_20181129_0024b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Zaydism, also called Fiver Shi'ism, is one of the three main branches of Shia Islam, the others being Twelverism and Ismailism, and is sometimes counted as a fifth school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> law. It emerged in the eighth century after Zayd ibn Ali's unsuccessful rebellion against the Umayyad caliph Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik. Most early Shia recognised Zayd's brother, Muhammad al-Baqir, as the fifth leader, but some regarded Zayd as the fifth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, and these formed the Zaydi or Fiver offshoot.</p><p>Zaydis are typically considered the Shia branch closest to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>. They regard rationalism as more important than Quranic literalism, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> they have historically been tolerant towards Shafi'i Sunnism. The classical form of Zaydism, called Hadawi, changed its stance towards Sunni and Shia traditions several times. The earliest form was Jarudiyya, and many of the first Zaydi states followed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Zaydi dynasties ruled in several regions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty</a> governed the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> from 788, and another Zaydi dynasty ruled in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the 11th century. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-zayd">Hasan ibn Zayd</a> the Alavids founded a Zaydi state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in 864, which lasted until its leader was killed by the Sunni Samanids in 928. It was revived about forty years later in Daylam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> and survived until 1126. The Karkiya dynasty ruled eastern Gilan from the 1370s to 1592, and the Zaydis of the Caspian region were forcibly converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'ism</a> in the 16th century.</p><p>Around 893 al-Hadi ila'l-Haqq Yahya, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-ali">Hasan ibn Ali</a>, established himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saada">Sa'dah</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and founded the Rassid dynasty. The Zaydi imamate there was re-established under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasim-ibn-muhammad">al-Mansur al-Qasim</a> after an Ottoman invasion in the 16th century, and a new line began in the 19th century with Muhammad bin Yahya Hamid ad-Din. From the 15th century Zaydi doctrine merged gradually with elements of Shafi'i Sunnism, and by the mid-18th century the Qasimi imams had become a dynasty. These two lines ruled Yemen, with minor interruptions, until the Yemen Arab Republic was created in 1962.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Most of the world's Zaydis live in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najran">Najran</a> in Saudi Arabia. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> in 1962 some Zaydis in northern Yemen converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>, and the loss of imamic rule led a number of Zaydi scholars to call for its restoration. The North Yemen Civil War lasted from 1962 to 1970. Zaydi revivalist movements later arose from the discontent of the community.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zaydism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hasan al-Utrush, known as al-Nasir al-Kabir, restored the state in 914 and converted many Daylamites and Gilites to <b>Zaydi Islam</b>, writing works of law still respected by Zaydis.</blockquote><a href="/state/alavid-dynasty">Alavid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>They long followed <b>Zaydi Shia Islam</b>, later adopting Twelver Shi'ism, and fought the Sunni Ishaqid dynasty of western Gilan.</blockquote><a href="/state/karkiya-dynasty">Karkiya dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Later sources describe them as <b>Zaydi Shi'is</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ukhaydirid-emirate">Ukhaydirid Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
